/**
 * @page DatabaseDesign Database Design
 *
 * @brief Database structure for storing rainwater monitoring data.
 *
 * The database stores sensor measurements received from LoRaWAN
 * nodes installed at rainwater buffering locations. In addition
 * to sensor values, radio communication metadata is stored to
 * evaluate the quality of the LoRaWAN connection.
 *
 *
 * @section DesignChoices Design Choices
 *
 * The following design choices were made during the database design:
 *
 * - Device information, sensor measurements and radio metadata
 *   are stored in separate tables.
 *
 * - Each device is assigned a unique internal identifier that
 *   serves as the primary key.
 *
 * - The Device EUI is stored separately and uniquely identifies
 *   each LoRaWAN node.
 *
 * - Measurements are linked to devices through a foreign key,
 *   establishing a one-to-many relationship.
 *
 * - Radio metadata is linked to individual measurements through
 *   a foreign key, allowing information from one or more gateways
 *   to be stored for a single uplink.
 *
 * - Timestamps are stored with each measurement to support
 *   historical analysis and trend monitoring.
 *
 * - Indexes are used on device identifiers and timestamps to
 *   improve query performance.
 *
 * - Cascading deletion is enabled to ensure that removing a
 *   device automatically removes all associated measurements
 *   and radio metadata.
 *
 *
 * @section EntityDescription Entity Description
 *
 * Device
 * - Stores information about a LoRaWAN node.
 * - Contains the Device ID, Device EUI and installation location.
 *
 * Measurement
 * - Stores sensor readings received from a device.
 * - Records battery voltage, water level and flow volume.
 * - Stores the timestamp of the received uplink.
 *
 * RadioMetadata
 * - Stores LoRaWAN reception information.
 * - Records the receiving gateway.
 * - Stores RSSI and SNR values used to assess radio link quality.
 * - Optionally stores gateway location information.
 *
 *
 * @section UMLDiagram Database UML Diagram
 *
 * @startuml
 *
 * class Device {
 *     <<table>>
 *     +PK id : int
 *     +device_id : char(16)
 *     +device_eui : char(16) {unique}
 *     +location : varchar(100)
 * }
 *
 * class Measurement {
 *     <<table>>
 *     +PK id : bigint
 *     +FK device_id : int
 *     +uplink_at : datetime
 *     +battery_voltage_cv : decimal(8,3)
 *     +flow_volume_ml : decimal(12,3)
 *     +level_cm : decimal(12,3)
 * }
 *
 * class RadioMetadata {
 *     <<table>>
 *     +PK id : bigint
 *     +FK measurement_id : bigint
 *     +gateway_name : varchar(100)
 *     +gateway_eui : char(16)
 *     +rssi : int
 *     +snr : decimal(5,2)
 *     +latitude : decimal(10,7)
 *     +longitude : decimal(10,7)
 *     +height : decimal(8,2)
 * }
 *
 * Device "1" -- "0..*" Measurement : generates
 * Measurement "1" -- "0..*" RadioMetadata : received by
 *
 * @enduml
 *
 *
 * @section Relationship Relationship Between Tables
 *
 * The database models the LoRaWAN monitoring network using two
 * one-to-many relationships:
 *
 * @verbatim
 *
 * Device (1) ----------- (*) Measurement
 *                            |
 *                            |
 *                            +------ (*) RadioMetadata
 *
 * @endverbatim
 *
 * A device generates many measurements over time. Each
 * measurement may be received by one or more gateways,
 * resulting in one or more radio metadata records.
 *
 *
 * @section StoredData Stored Data
 *
 * The database stores the following information:
 *
 * - Device identification.
 * - Device installation location.
 * - Measurement timestamp.
 * - Battery voltage (cV).
 * - Water level (cm).
 * - Flow volume (mL).
 * - Gateway identifier.
 * - Gateway name.
 * - RSSI (Received Signal Strength Indicator).
 * - SNR (Signal-to-Noise Ratio).
 * - Gateway location data.
 *
 * This information allows analysis of both the rainwater
 * buffering system and the performance of the LoRaWAN
 * communication network.
 *
 * @see UseCase
 * @see ApplicationArchitecture
 */